#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128838 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07b63c0c-daaa-5f5b-acb3-58d6f36ee869" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "ba2819ea-837b-5cfd-9475-8ed5a7bda000" :question/math-academy-id "e-3582" :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ plotted below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/145658c34dbb00034b6a487c5e77e888.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=0$. Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(0) = -2$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = -2, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = 2\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=0$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there are also discontinuities at $x=-3$ and $x=3$, but they are removable discontinuities, *not* jump discontinuities."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128839 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02297ca-0132-591b-9a69-bc2f93c95e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Jump Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuity Exist"}] :question/id #uuid "3607c9c1-25ca-5cfc-bd15-66e3b7e504c2" :question/math-academy-id "e-3583" :question/problem "For which points does the function $y=f(x)$ below have a jump discontinuity?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/c6c40035c03ae2873bb952c1aba81138.png)" :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=2$. Checking our continuity conditions, we have\n\n- $f(2)= 1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- $\\displaystyle \\lim_{x\\to 2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 2^-}f(x) = -1, \\qquad \\qquad \\lim_{x\\to 2^+}f(x) = 1\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nNote that there is also a discontinuity at $x=1$, but this is not a jump discontinuity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128848 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "485fd759-96b3-553f-9eaa-849878c30885" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity Given a Function Definition"}] :question/id #uuid "666caa80-54d7-5a3c-9ab9-1057e3fdeb90" :question/math-academy-id "e-3671" :question/problem "Does the function $f(x)$ below have a jump discontinuity at $x=0$?\n\n${x^{2} + 1,\\begin{vmatrix}x \\le 0 \\\\ 2x - 1,\\end{vmatrix}x > 0$" :question/worked-solution "Let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(0)=1$ exists $\\,\\,{\\color{green}{\\checkmark}}$\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0^-}f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to 0^-} 2x-1 = 2(0)-1 = -1$\nwhile\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}f(x) = \\lim_{x\\to 0^+} x^2+1 = 0^2+1 = 1$.\nSo both the left and right limits exist and are finite, but they are not equal. $\\,\\,{\\color{red}{\\times}}$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d02297ca-0132-591b-9a69-bc2f93c95e2f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Jump Discontinuities on a Graph when Multiple Types of Discontinuity Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c8e4bae-40f9-57da-a3da-ff0669f18324" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128211} {:answer/id #uuid "3b22443a-3143-5730-93f6-8ddcba6d2ec5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-3$, $x = 0$ and $x = 1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128212} {:answer/id #uuid "4811bea4-80b9-55af-8dc6-9ae022c5adb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-3$ and $x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128213} {:answer/id #uuid "53d65e16-a75a-5231-91dd-7a5156be42ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128214} {:answer/id #uuid "69b09b63-8d50-5bff-a999-f9151f885616" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4811bea4-80b9-55af-8dc6-9ae022c5adb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-3$ and $x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128213} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce30e210-27f9-5333-a2f4-32f325604d71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128210}] :question/id #uuid "2ed69968-e37f-592e-8251-96c027066333" :question/math-academy-id "q-48119" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/q-48119.png)\n\nFor which points does the function $y = f(x)$ above have a jump discontinuity?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "07b63c0c-daaa-5f5b-acb3-58d6f36ee869" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb6cf62e-f836-5d83-93d7-91f033ea47af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/q-48141-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128229} {:answer/id #uuid "f744a4a4-3f83-5c2f-a569-c5509fec0768" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2003/Source/Images/q-48141-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186128230} {:answer/id #uuid "29f5b25b-8f02-550a-91ac-2fcfadfd9a18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/